{"text": "In the valley where two rivers braided like silver threads, there lived a young carpenter named Liko. He carved boats from driftwood that washed ashore after storms, believing each piece carried a memory of the sea. One evening, as the lanterns flickered, an old woman cloaked in kelp-silk visited his workshop. Her eyes held the color of tide pools, and she spoke in a whisper that sounded like shells rubbing together. “Your work is honest,” she said, “yet you fear letting the river guide you.” She handed him a shard of green glass from a lighthouse lamp that had long since gone dim. “When you place this shard in the heart of a boat, it will reveal the voyage others cannot see.” Curious, Liko embedded the shard into the keel of a stout oarless skiff.

The next morning, the river woke with a new voice. The boats he built whispered directions, not to the shore but to the people who needed them most: a widow who lost her husband at sea, a child who could not find his way home, a fisherman who forgot the taste of rain. Each voyage became a thread mending a village unstitched by fear.

From then on, Liko’s harbor hummed with trust, for every boat carried a memory, and every memory steered a future toward kindness."}

